/**

@page page_installation_with_docker Installation with Docker

Here we maintain Dockerfiles for different compilers.
They reuse the Spack environments stored under `utilities/spack`.

## Usage

<div style="color:Black ; background-color: #ccf ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
\par 🍎 Running on Apple Silicon (ARM) Macs
Our images are built for `linux/amd64`, but they run on M-series Macs through Docker
Desktop's built-in emulation. Just add `--platform linux/amd64` to your `docker run`
command, for example:
```shell
docker run --platform linux/amd64 -it -v ${PWD}:/work --rm --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```
</div>

For users, it is recommended to pull the available Docker images from [Dockerhub](https://hub.docker.com/u/peanoframework).
All Docker images that have the extension `gcc`, `intel`, `nvidia`, `llvm`, or `amd`
provide all commonly the same dependencies that are needed to build, run and visualise Peano applications.
All dependencies within the images are installed via Spack.
Each image additional provides one of the compilers with MPI that are also configured for GPU offloading.
You may choose any image to pull from Dockerhub that fits your needs in a compiler and your hardware configuration.

You may ignore images that have the extension `base`, or `spack`. These are only used for development.

To use the prebuilt images, we do recommended that you first clone Peano, then navigate into the Peano checkout
and run the Docker container interactively by mapping the Peano directory into the container (here, we use `work` as our mapping point):

```shell
docker run -it -v ${PWD}:/work --rm --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```

### With GPU Support

```shell
docker run -it -v ${PWD}:/work --rm --gpus=all --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```

### With Port Forwarding (JupyterLab)

```shell
docker run -it -v ${PWD}:/work -p 8888:8888 --rm --privileged peanoframework/<compiler>
```

and then you can run

```shell
jupyter lab --allow-root --port=8888 --no-browser --ip=0.0.0.0
```

## Build (for Developers)

The next part is mostly relevant for developers of the project or if you want to change or add some additionally dependencies
to the Docker images yourself. Keep in mind, that building the image takes a significant amount of time and requires root access.

To build the Docker images, make sure to execute the following commands in the Peano root directory.

### Building Base Images

```shell
docker build -t peanoframework/base -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.base .
docker build -t peanoframework/spack -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.spack .
```

### Building Compiler Images

```shell
docker build -t peanoframework/gcc -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.gcc .
docker build -t peanoframework/intel -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.intel .
docker build -t peanoframework/nvidia -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.nvidia .
docker build -t peanoframework/amd -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.amd .
docker build -t peanoframework/llvm -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.llvm .
```

### Building Extension Images

```shell
docker build -t peanoframework/exahype2 -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.exahype2 .
docker build -t peanoframework/exahype2-tutorials -f utilities/docker/Dockerfile.exahype2-tutorials .
```

*/
